window.STORY_SOURCE = [{"key":"question","title":"The question and shared framework","paragraphCount":20,"tableCount":0,"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Introduction"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Looking back at the vast expanse of human history, I find myself inevitably drawn to seek the roots of the present, tracing lines that connect antiquity to modern realities. Yet this connection, whether in practice or in theory, is often overlooked, with different eras treated as isolated laboratories sealed off from one another. Presented with a law passed during the Roman Republic, even specialists might struggle to explain why it should matter to modern America. Yet this rigid compartmentalization of timelines causes us to overlook recurring patterns that persist across time and space. By juxtaposing two seemingly disparate urban landscapes — the neighborhoods of the late Roman Republic and early Empire, and the grid of modern San Francisco between 1937 and 2000 — this paper uncovers a blueprint of human division shared by both cities. Specifically, both demonstrate how the allocation of resources, particularly infrastructure and capital investment, functions as a powerful tool for shaping social segregation, revealing an ancient echo of the modern phenomenon known as \"redlining.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"My motivation for this research emerged from two distinct intellectual paths that collided unexpectedly. Years of immersive study in Latin and classical culture drew me away from idealized mythology and toward the lived realities of Roman society. I began by examining the Greco-Roman world through a feminist lens, analyzing medical practices used by women to understand how marginalized groups survived under systemic segregation. At the same time, as a resident of San Francisco, I witnessed firsthand the friction of modern urban transformation. This lived experience led me to research gentrification in some of the city's most disadvantaged neighborhoods, where I traced how twentieth-century policy continues to dictate who thrives in a community and who is displaced from it."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The research project is formed from a profound curiosity to discover how deep these architectural and societal similarities run. Comparing the strictly classified society of ancient Rome with the formally graded, financially stratified neighborhoods of twentieth-century San Francisco, this paper argues that inequality is not merely a matter of prejudice or individual bias, but a structural condition constructed through the government control of infrastructure and capital. In Rome, access to water, such as proximity to baths, fountains, or the presence of a private pipe grant, was mapped in consideration of the rigid hierarchy of the ordines:  the citizen body's ranked legal classes. In San Francisco, access to mortgage capital and highway routing was determined by boundaries drawn on the 1937 Home Owners' Loan Corporation (HOLC) map. Neither comparison claims that Roman water politics and American redlining are interchangeable events. On the other hand, both share a common basis: an authority deciding who receives a resource, a decision that entrenched perpetuating hierarchy into the culture itself."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A comparison across two thousand years and two continents raises a question: without any direct historical link between the Roman Republic and twentieth-century San Francisco, is the pairing simply an arbitrary decision? According to the historian Marc Bloch, a comparative method offers an answer to this problem. Bloch argued that when a phenomenon (A) is attributed to a specific condition (B), the strength of that causal claim can be tested by searching for other instances where A appears in the absence of B (Sewell, 1967). If no such instance can be found, confidence that A and B are structurally linked rises accordingly (Sewell, 1967). This logic does not require the cases under comparison to be geographically or temporally adjacent. If anything, the absence of proximity strengthens the test. Since two societies with no contact and no possibility of borrowing a practice from the other they cannot be said to demonstrate the same mechanisms by inheritance or coincidental influence. Applying such reasoning to Rome and San Francisco, this absence of connection makes the convergence of the same underlying problem between them meaningful. The decisions to allocate scarce resources like capital investment and infrastructure across a segregated population are the conjunction points of how hierarchical societies manage resource distribution."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This paper situates itself within the broader tradition of comparative urbanism, which asks not whether cities are alike in fine details, but whether the procedure by which they organize space, resources, and belonging recur across radically different historical contexts. By tracing this mechanism across two thousand years, this paper contends that segregation is best understood not as a series of isolated policies, but as a recurring theme of exclusion, built and rebuilt in every era through the infrastructure a society chooses to share and withhold."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This paper proceeds in four parts. I first introduce the project within prior research on Roman urban topography and twentieth-century American HOLC policy, identifying where the relevant data for each city can be found and the background information required to analyze resource distribution and segregation in each context individually. I then turn to data analysis, describing how the Roman and San Franciscan datasets used in this study were assembled. I then explain and introduce calculations used to translate inequality across these times and regions. Finally, I review tables displaying results in each case. The findings section presents the relationships between modern San Francisco city planning and the regions in ancient Rome, transferring the HOLC grading system as a blueprint to evaluate the neighborhoods of Rome. I close by considering what this comparison leaves open, and where the method developed here might be extended in future research."},{"type":"heading","text":"Constructing Hierarchy"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Before resources can be allocated unequally, a society first needs a system for ranking entitlement of certain individuals. This rating of categories makes unequal treatment seem procedural rather than arbitrary. Rome and the United States built this mechanism through different vocabularies and structures, but both used it to convert social difference into a durable, administrative hierarchy."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Ancient Rome did not classify people along biological categories as we see today, instead being a more fluid and ideological system that classifies rank, thus ultimately justifying which people had dominance over others (Radin, 1923). According to a sample research, out of nearly 14,000 inscriptions on tombs, almost 83 percent of those born in Rome fall into categories the epigraphic evidence marks as foreign in lineage (Frank, 1916). This implies that Rome had an extremely diverse society, questioning how Romans conceptually organize difference. An article studying “proto-racism” in the Graeco-Roman world wrote that human difference in antiquity represented a realistic and influential mode of thought and drew on several explanations to account for segregation between people; environmental determinism, inheritance of acquired traits, a combination of these two, and, rarely, pure lineage (Isaac, 2006)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A more productive framework for this topic would treat “race” in ancient Roman as an ideological construct for differentiating and ranking groups. A central idea is “Othering”; manufacturing traits in foreign peoples and marking those traits as evidence of inferiority, danger, or otherness. Through various words like gens, genus, and populus, Romans reflected a strictly hierarchical system for categorizing outsiders rather than a simple racial taxonomy. In the process of “Othering,” physical appearances sometimes play a role – the unusual height of the Gauls, the \"large and powerful bodies\" of the Germans, the body paint of the Britons. Yet character traits were often more defining. Recorded by authors from the Republic to the late Empire, the rhetoric gradually hardened into a precise classification of “barbarity.” Climate theory evolved along with this stereotype, with early authors like Caesar barely mentioning environment as a factor (Susalla, 2014). Yet later writers such as Pliny and Tacitus directly linked climate to body type and character traits. Together, these articles support an argument that Roman \"race\" functioned as a dynamic ideological and political system for ranking social difference rather than as a fixed, biologically based category as in modern racial classification."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This ideological system had a legal counterpart in the Roman concept of equality, which evolved from a limited civic ideal into a profound system of legal inequality where social standing determined access to justice and protection. Initially, during the Republic, the idea of equality was a vague structure within the citizen body (civis), commonly characterized through equal division of war spoils (viritim) rather than distribution by rank. However, this egalitarian ideal excluded women, children, freedmen, slaves, foreigners, and even free adult male citizens, who lacked full legal rights due to the absolute authority of patria potestas as long as their fathers were alive. The internal civic equality faded rapidly over time through the early division between patricians and plebeians, eventually evolving into ordines (ranked social classes). Recorded on an official list by the censor, the rigid classes determined that equality did not expand along with the augmentation of citizenship (to more ethnicities). Through this influence, social standing became a legal determination that dictated whether an individual could sue a social superior or receive police protection (Radin, 1923)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"By the Antonine Period (around 200CE), Roman society was explicitly divided into two levels: the privileged upper orders (honestiores) and everyone else (humiliores). Legal penalties and punishments became entirely based on this social classification rather than the crime itself. For example, while honestiores were exempt from flogging and torture, humiliores were subjected to brutal punishments, including forced labor in the mines and death. Ultimately, real equality was inexistent in Roman antiquity. It only served the privileged minority on the summit of the social pyramid in this strictly hierarchical society (Radin, 1923; Garnsey, 1968)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The United States constructed a similar structure in the twentieth century, though its classification was explicitly racial rather than juridical. The federal government's Home Owners' Loan Corporation (HOLC) built its neighborhood ratings directly to the presence of Black residents and other groups it labeled undesirable, converting racial composition into quantified categories like the way Rome's censors converted status into recorded rank (Greer, 2014). In both systems, an act of classification shifted the hierarchy into what looks like historical record-keeping."},{"type":"heading","text":"Allocation of resources within hierarchy"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"While the previous discussion explained how each society decided who ranked where, this section examines how that ranking was institutionalized, specifically expressed through which neighborhoods received water, and which received capital. Roman water infrastructure was not only about universal drinking access but also which amenities a given source of water could unlock. Basic household water for most Romans, commonly the residents inside insulae, came from wells and cisterns rather than the aqueducts, with groundwater rather than river water functioning as the city's real base resource. This pattern can be directly connected to the modern groundwater usage in America. Aqueduct construction was instead driven primarily by bathing,  called \"the greatest single reason\" the system was built at all (Deming, 2020). Rome's eleven major aqueducts (constructed between 312 BCE and roughly 200–226 CE) delivered enormous volumes by ancient standards. For example, Frontinus records 14,018 quinariae daily across nine aqueducts, translating to a range of roughly 84 to 200 million gallons per day depending on how that unit is interpreted (Morgan, 1902; Frontinus, 97CE)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The clearest case study of this bathing-driven infrastructure is Agrippa's Thermae: it was private when built in 25 BCE, but later expanded into a full bathing complex (frigidarium, tepidarium, caldarium, plus the massive Stagnum Agrippae pool) which was made possible by the Aqua Virgo (completed in 19 BCE specifically to feed the Campus Martius with a small number of high-capacity outlets). Following Agrippa's death, the baths became free to the public in perpetuity (Kontokosta, 2019). It was a calculated act of political generosity which evolved to a stage where within those baths, status distinctions are reported to have genuinely dissolved, with emperors and ordinary subjects bathing side by side. What determined class, on the other hand, was the presence of fountains inside specific households either drawn from aqueducts or planned with groundwater. These occasions often occurred under bribery of officers (aquarii) by citizens of high status and displayed inequality at its greatest extent."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The American parallel to this system is redlining, whose structural origins lie in the New Deal response to the Great Depression. In 1933, in the midst of a nationwide crisis, Congress passed the Home Owners' Loan Act, establishing HOLC as an emergency agency to refinance defaulted mortgages and keep struggling homeowners in their homes. Between 1933 and 1936, HOLC's initial \"rescue phase\" issued more than one million low-interest, long-term loans. This was directed to the majority (over 97 percent) who purchases and refinances mortgages for homeowners facing bank foreclosure. Notably, this early rescue lending was extended to Black homeowners as well as white ones (Greer, 2014). The agency changed in its second, \"consolidation\" phase, beginning around 1935."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Tasked by its parent body, the Federal Home Loan Bank Board, having gained better understanding of the loans, HOLC launched the City Survey Program, sending field agents into 239 cities to interview local lenders and real estate brokers and compile neighborhood-by-neighborhood risk appraisals. The result was a set of \"Residential Security Maps\". These were color-coded on a four-tier scale: green (\"A,\" best), blue (\"B,\" still desirable), yellow (\"C,\" declining), and red (\"D,\" hazardous), engendering the origin of the term \"redlining.\" These grades were not based on housing stock and terrain alone. HOLC's own appraisal manuals instructed surveyors to weigh the \"threat of infiltration\" of foreign-born, Black, or otherwise racially or ethnically undesirable residents, and area-description reports from the period record this reasoning explicitly, citing the presence of Black or immigrant residents as grounds for a lower grade even in neighborhoods with sound housing stock (Nelson, 2023)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"San Francisco was among the 239 cities surveyed, receiving its own Residential Security Map in 1937. As in other cities, the lowest grades were clustered in neighborhoods with concentrations of low-income residents, immigrants, and residents of color, while majority-white, higher-income neighborhoods were graded favorably regardless of housing quality. The following year, the Federal Housing Administration adopted similar racial criteria in its own mortgage insurance program, extending the practice beyond HOLC's own lending and embedding it in the private mortgage industry for decades. Although HOLC itself was formally ended and its maps eventually discredited, historians continue to debate exactly how much accurate independent weight the maps carried rather than how much they simply codified lending patterns that already existed. What is not contested is the outcome: the effects of this classification persisted long after the policy itself was reversed. Recent studies find that neighborhoods HOLC graded \"hazardous\" in the 1930s remain disproportionately low-to-moderate income and disproportionately home to residents of color today, with measurable disparities in basic walkable amenities such as markets and exercise facilities. To trace this legacy in San Francisco specifically, this paper draws on Mapping Inequality datasets and public registries to observe capital investment decisions against the boundaries HOLC drew nearly a century ago (Duncan, 2012)."}]},{"key":"sanfrancisco","title":"San Francisco and HOLC grading","paragraphCount":24,"tableCount":0,"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"San Francisco and the Home Owner’s Loan Corporation"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Home Owners' Loan Corporation (HOLC) appraisals of San Francisco neighborhoods for 1937 are available online through the University of Richmond’s Mapping Inequality site. They include geojson files to draw the historical boundaries of neighborhoods and regions of the city. They also include detailed descriptions of these areas that provided greater insights into their topologies, economics, and demographics (Nelson, 2023). In all 96 regions were outlined. As with other HOLC maps, they divided the city into four distinct grades: A, B, C, and D. Each of these grades reflected a combination of physical, economic, and social factors. These grades, often color-coded as green (A), blue (B), yellow (C), and red (D), were not merely reflections of housing quality but also of the perceived stability, desirability, and social composition of each area."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In this section districts related to each of these grades will be analyzed to extract commonalities that describe when and where a property may be considered more or less desirable for investment. These trends will then be used to help code for details in the Pleiades dataset. To approximate HOLC grades for regions of Augustan Rome. For simplicity the dollar amounts for home valuations and incomes are listed in their original amounts. Inflation adjusted numbers are available upon request."},{"type":"heading","text":"A Districts"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"San Francisco’s A Districts were the most prestigious and exclusive neighborhoods. Accounting for 13 of the 96 areas and 8.65% of the graded footprint, these districts were characterized by high property values, strict zoning, and a homogeneous, affluent population. As one appraisal notes, the Sea Clift neighborhood was \"highly restricted to single-family residences, both by ordinance and deed.\" The area was \"inhabited largely by professional and business men of the executive type, having incomes ranging from $4,000 to $40,000.\" The sense of exclusivity was reinforced in unsubtle ways that extended beyond economics. This included concerns about preventing the \"infiltration of inharmonious racial elements.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Homes in A districts were often large, architecturally significant, and meticulously maintained. For example, in one area, \"all improvements are single-family dwellings of the mansion type, ranging from $15,000 to $50,000 in cost.\" The neighborhoods were almost entirely owner-occupied, with very few vacancies, and residents enjoyed proximity to parks, schools, and recreational areas. The appraisals frequently emphasized the absence of \"undesirable social elements\" and the presence of deed restrictions and zoning that protected the area’s character."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A districts also benefited from natural advantages, such as scenic views and seclusion including \"a rolling plateau which rises to a height of more than 300 feet...furnishing an inspiring view of the 'Golden Gate' and the mountains beyond.\" The stability of these neighborhoods was seen as virtually unassailable. Mortgage lenders viewed A district areas as the safest investments, and property values remained resilient even during economic downturns. As another appraisal put it, \"Owing to its natural advantages and the other factors mentioned, it is quite possible that this district will remain the outstanding residential district of San Francisco for a great many years to come.\""},{"type":"heading","text":"B Districts"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"B Districts represented stable, middle-class neighborhoods that were \"still desirable.\" Covering 30 of the 96 districts and 31.32% of the graded area, they featured moderate to high property values and a mix of housing types, including single-family homes, flats, and small apartment buildings. The population was predominantly white and middle-class, with residents described as \"professional and business people, 'white collar' workers, skilled artisans, and junior executives.” While not as affluent as those in grade A districts, the residents had solid annual incomes in the  “$1,800 to $7,500\" range."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Owner-occupancy rates in B districts were high, often between 70% and 95%, and maintenance standards were generally good. The neighborhoods were well-served by schools, parks, and public transportation, and many benefited from proximity to amenities such as Golden Gate Park or the ocean. The HOLC appraisals often noted the absence of \"adverse racial concentrations,\" with one stating, \"there are no racial concentrations, and the possibility of there being any is remote.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"While B districts were considered stable, some were seen as being at or near their peak, with the potential for future decline. Mortgage lending was generally favorable, though some institutions were cautious in areas bordering lower-graded districts or with older housing stock. The variance in value across B districts was greater for those on the periphery of a B district clustering. Of note \"there are spotted blocks here and there which could be designated 'high yellow', and on the other hand, there are scattered blocks...which could be classed as 'low green.'\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"B districts also included neighborhoods with a mix of new and older developments. For instance, one area was described as \"the city's newest restricted, single-family residential sub-division,\" with \"no building in this area older than five years.\" The presence of Federal Housing Administration (FHA) loans was noted as a factor in stimulating development. In other B districts, the influence of nearby parks, scenic views, and well-maintained business centers contributed to their desirability. However, some B areas were considered less popular or at risk of decline due to proximity to lower-income neighborhoods or less favorable locations."},{"type":"heading","text":"C Districts"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"C Districts, or \"yellow\" areas, were labeled as \"definitely declining.\" Covering 26 of the 96 districts and 24.97% of the graded area, these neighborhoods were marked by aging and heterogeneous housing stock, lower middle-class and working-class populations, and increasing signs of obsolescence. Appraisals described these areas as having \"a heterogeneous mixture of single and multiple family dwellings, flats and apartment houses, having a cost range of $3,500 to $10,000 and an average age of from 10 to 20 years, many of the buildings being fully 25 years old.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Residents of C districts included those with incomes from $1,200 to $4,000. Owner-occupancy rates were lower than in A or B districts, often between 60% and 80%, and maintenance was variable. One appraisal noted, \"maintenance is spotted and shows only a fair degree of pride of ownership.\" The threat of \"undesirable\" racial infiltration was frequently cited as a risk, citing “a distinct threat of infiltration of Negroes and Japs from areas D-1 and D-3.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"C districts often bordered industrial areas, railroads, or older commercial corridors, which were seen as negative influences. Environmental issues such as fog, wind, or lack of sunshine were also mentioned as detrimental. Mortgage lending was more conservative in these areas, with institutions often restricting terms or declining to lend in certain neighborhoods. As one appraisal put it, \"loan applications in this area are closely scrutinized and are usually made upon a very conservative basis.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Despite these challenges, some C districts showed signs of potential improvement, particularly where new construction was taking place or where proximity to parks and amenities offered hope for revitalization. However, the overall tone was one of caution and concern about further decline. The appraisals often highlighted the inharmonious development and obsolescence of housing stock, with one noting, \"the development of the area, which began some 30 years ago, has been heterogeneous in the extreme, and while the improvements consist largely of single-family residences of mixed types …there are also numerous apartment houses and flats.\""},{"type":"heading","text":"D Districts"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"D Districts, or \"red\" areas, were considered \"hazardous\" and represented the least desirable neighborhoods. Covering 17 of the 96 districts and the remaining 35.06% of the HOLC grading, these areas were characterized by low property values, poor housing conditions, and high concentrations of marginalized populations. One appraisal described a D district as \"the nearest approach to a slum district in San Francisco …consisting of Japanese, Russians, Mexicans, Negroes, … having a very low income level.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Housing in D districts was often old, overcrowded, and in poor repair. \"The single-family residences had a cost range when new of from $3,500 to $7,000 and consist mainly of frame structures having an average age of from 30 to 50 years.” Housing had lower owner occupancy combined with higher vacancy rates. The appraisals frequently cited the presence of \"undesirable racial elements\" as a major factor in the area's low rating, with one stating, \"more than half the Negro population of San Francisco are located here.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"D districts were often adjacent to industrial areas, railroads, or other sources of pollution and noise. Due to perceived lower sense of pride due to lower ownership rates, maintenance was generally poor. Mortgage lending was extremely limited, with most institutions refusing to make loans except on the basis of land value and only then on very restrictive terms. As one appraisal bluntly put it, \"only one mortgagee institution was found that would accept applications … largely as a matter of principle.\""},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Despite these challenges, some D districts showed evidence of pride of ownership among certain residents, and a few areas were noted as having potential for future improvement. However, the overall picture was one of neglect, poverty, and exclusion from investment and opportunity. The appraisals often described these areas as being in a state of decline, with little hope for recovery without significant intervention."},{"type":"heading","text":"Factors Accounting for District Coding"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The HOLC grading system in San Francisco reflected and reinforced patterns of social stratification, economic opportunity, and racial segregation. A districts were exclusive enclaves of wealth and privilege, protected by restrictive covenants and social homogeneity. B districts offered stability and comfort to the middle class, while C districts faced the challenges of aging infrastructure and social change. D districts, marked by poverty, ethnic diversity, and neglect, were systematically denied access to credit and investment. The factors that accounted for these differences shaped the urban landscape for decades, with lasting consequences for the city's development and its residents. Of particular relevance were property values, zoning, income, race, maintenance, greenspaces, and environmental hazards. In the following section, these factors will be translated to approximate HOLC grades to Augustan Rome."}]},{"key":"rome","title":"Rome regions and archaeological dataset","paragraphCount":55,"tableCount":0,"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Data Collection and Details"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In this section I will use the Pleiades geographic dataset in an attempt to map the  historical real estate grading system of 1930s San Francisco onto the 14 administrative regions of Augustan Rome. By leveraging Pleiades' structured coordinates and location metadata, ancient archaeological ruins are mapped to each of the regions. To replicate the federal HOLC color-coded boundaries, multiple methods will be explored. Case selection criteria, valence assignment, and statistical approaches are combined to analyze the distribution of factors such as luxury housing and environmental hazards across the map."},{"type":"heading","text":"Understanding the Pleiades Dataset"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Pleiades dataset, hosted by the Institute for the Study of the Ancient World at New York University and originating with the Stoa Consortium, is a community-built digital gazetteer and graph of ancient places. Unlike traditional maps or physical history books that provide flat, unchanging snapshots of historical geography, Pleiades acts as an authoritative, continuously updated network database. It is designed to catalog, connect, and preserve information about how humans interacted with spaces in the ancient world. It is used by researchers, online search engines, and advanced data visualization tools to map historical events and cross-reference geographic information across different digital humanities projects."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The geographic and temporal scope of Pleiades is massive, making it a critical tool for studying ancient history. Geographically, it covers most of Europe, North Africa, the Mediterranean basin, the Near East, and parts of Central and Southwest Asia. You can find data on everything from major imperial capitals like Rome or Constantinople to tiny, isolated rural villages, desert forts, mountain passes, and ancient rivers. Temporally, the dataset stretches far beyond just the classical Greek and Roman eras. It includes geographic data starting from the Bronze Age and the ancient Near East, moving forward through the classical and Hellenistic periods, the Roman Empire, and into late antiquity and the early Byzantine and Islamic eras. This broad timescale allows researchers to track how cities grew, changed names, or completely disappeared over thousands of years of human history."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"To capture all of this historical complexity, the Pleiades dataset relies on a highly structured database schema composed of specific, relevant fields grouped into three core concepts: Places, Names, and Locations."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Places: A \"Place\" is the main baseline object in the database, representing a distinct spatial concept or entity (like a city, a mountain, or a road network) rather than just a single point on a map. Relevant fields within a Place record include a unique identifier (URI) for permanent digital referencing, a primary title, a feature type classification (such as \"settlement,\" \"fort,\" \"quarry,\" or \"river\"), and a general text description explaining the historical background."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Names: Because ancient places were called different things by different cultures over time, the \"Names\" fields handle the linguistic history of a site. These fields capture the name string in its native script (like Greek, Latin, or Arabic), its transliterated forms, the language code, and the temporal period during which that specific name was actively used."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Locations: The \"Locations\" fields anchor the place to physical reality. These fields store precise modern geographic coordinates using latitude and longitude (often formatted as GeoJSON geometry objects). Crucially, this section also includes accuracy and data-certainty fields, telling researchers whether a coordinate is exactly verified by modern archaeology or just an educated guess based on ancient texts."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Metadata & Provenance: Finally, every entry contains fields for bibliographic references, digital citations, and a chronological history of edits made by the scholarly community, ensuring full transparency."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The primary usages of the Pleiades dataset are foundational to modern digital archaeology and historical research. In college research and professional academia, it serves as a central lookup reference tool. If an undergraduate student or a professor reads an obscure ancient city name in a text by Herodotus or Pliny the Elder, they can use Pleiades to find out exactly where that city was located and what it is called today. Beyond simple searches, Pleiades acts as a crucial \"linked data\" hub for the internet. Huge historical projects use its unique identifier URLs to link their own data collections together. For instance, an online museum can link its collection of ancient coins straight to Pleiades place records, while a digital library can link its collection of digitized manuscripts to those same records. This data interconnection lets researchers map out ancient trade routes, analyze how wealth was distributed across empires, or build interactive, time-scaled maps that visually demonstrate how urban inequality and geographic power shifted over thousands of years."},{"type":"heading","text":"Mapping the Data"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"To firmly ground the methodology of tracking spatial inequality, it is critical to note that using linked geospatial data to map demographics and socioeconomic conditions to specific ancient coordinates has strong scholarly precedent. By utilizing the Pleiades dataset scholars can anchor historical artifact distributions and population proxy variables by location (Barker et al., 2016). For example, historical transport network densities and economic trade flows have been modeled directly against these digitized locations to analyze how urban development and wealth accumulation shifted relative to infrastructure access (De Soto, 2019). Furthermore, by isolating specific structural feature types within the dataset, researchers have successfully mapped regional socioeconomic disparities, population patterns, and systemic resource distribution gradients across ancient Rome (Romanello & Bodard, 2020). Prior literature using the Pleiades data gives us precedent to treat classical geography not merely as an atlas to observe passively, but as an empirical canvas to actively engage with for evaluating systemic historical socioeconomic inequality."},{"type":"heading","text":"Translating San Francisco to Augustan Rome"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"To set up this geographic project clearly, this study uses the famous archaeological maps drawn by Filippo Coarelli to establish the official boundaries of Rome's administrative regions. Coarelli (2007) created these 14 regional boundaries by matching ancient 4th-century text records with real physical ruins dug up by modern historians. Instead of drawing a clean, perfect circle around the city, Coarelli’s map captures the true, messy layout of ancient Rome. Some central neighborhoods were tiny and packed tight, while the outer edge zones sprawled across massive chunks of land."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"By using the Coarelli regional map as our baseline grid, we can roughly compare the layout of ancient Rome to the 1937 HOLC maps of San Francisco."},{"type":"heading","text":"The 14 Administrative Regions of Augustan Rome"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The 14 regions mapped provide the physical framework for a neighborhood comparison. The names and general descriptions of each region are outlined below:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio I: Porta Capena\nA sprawling, southern peripheral region located outside the city's ancient stone wall. It served as a busy traffic corridor centered around the Appian Way, packed with roadside tombs, traveler lodging, and water channels."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio II: Caelimontium\nA mixed-use hill district covering the Caelian Hill. The heights featured affluent estate homes and military barracks, while the lower slopes blended into high-density worker blocks and bustling markets."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio III: Isis et Serapis\nA compact valley and hillside district that contained the massive Colosseum valley. This region was a highly active entertainment and commercial hub, surrounded by multi-story apartment blocks for everyday workers."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio IV: Templum Pacis\nA heavily crowded, central valley region encompassing the infamous Subura district. It was a chaotic, high-density neighborhood where working-class market stalls and cheap wooden tenements stood directly alongside grand public monuments."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio V: Esquiliae\nA massive, sprawling eastern peripheral plain. This area acted as a major municipal edge zone, hosting vast public parks and elite estates on one side, while absorbing sprawling underclass cemeteries and defensive border walls on the other."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio VI: Alta Semita\nAn elevated, high-status hill district positioned along the Quirinal ridge. This region was a highly desirable residential zone, characterized by clean air, vast private garden estates, and premium villas away from the valley traffic."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio VII: Via Lata\nA stable, wealthy district flanking the wide northern highway. It served as a prime residential area filled with upscale townhouses, public porticoes, and direct access to clean aqueduct spring lines."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio VIII: Forum Romanum\nThe hyper-dense, monumental core of Roman public life. This tiny region was the administrative and political capital of the empire, heavily packed with grand public squares, courts, and temples, with zero space allowed for factories or residential trash."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio IX: Circus Flaminius\nA major public leisure and entertainment plain located along the Tiber river. It was packed with public plazas, theaters, temples, and open sports arenas, acting as a massive social hub for the entire city population."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio X: Palatium\nThe ultra-exclusive imperial hilltop core covering the Palatine Hill. This pinpoint region held a flat monopoly on absolute wealth, serving as the gated home of the emperor’s palaces and premium elite mansions, completely insulated from urban hazards."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio XI: Circus Maximus\nA long, narrow valley region dominated by the massive chariot-racing stadium. While it held immense utility and public value, it was a high-traffic zone constantly crowded by millions of spectators and commercial stalls."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio XII: Piscina Publica\nA quiet, residential southern hill district. It featured a healthy mix of public water reservoirs and standard housing blocks, acting as a comfortable, stable neighborhood for middle-class residents."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio XIII: Aventinus\nA starkly split riverside hill district. While the high slopes of the Aventine Hill hosted affluent townhouses, its low riverside flats contained the city's main shipping port, massive wholesale granaries, and intense commercial dock traffic."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Regio XIV: Trans Tiberim\nThe ultimate industrial sacrifice zone, separated from the city core across the Tiber River. This expansive peripheral region absorbed the city's heaviest burdens, legally concentrating all smoky brick factories, toxic leather tanneries, and deep stone quarries directly into the backyards of its dense laborer settlements."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In both cities, the natural landscape, hills, and walls were used to separate different social classes and protect property values.  When we look at the ruins inside the mapped regions, we see a clear rich-to-poor pattern that looks like San Francisco's color-coded grades. For example, the tight, high hilltop of the Palatine (Regio X) on Coarelli's map behaves like the exclusive, insulated heights of Pacific Heights. On the flip side, the large, low-lying outer plains (Regio V) and the basin across the river (Regio XIV) look like the industrial sacrifice zones of Bayview-Hunters Point or South of Market, which were redlined because the city dumped all its pollution and working-class housing there."},{"type":"heading","text":"Narrowing the Plaiedaes Set"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"To properly select cases for analysis a rough bounding box was first drawn around Rome. This covered the area roughly between 41.870° and 41.915° latitude and between 12.460° and 12.520° longitude."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"With this first filter applied the Pleiades data set provided 383 places to analyze. The next step required drawing geofence boundaries for the regions. Using google maps, approximate latitude-longitude polygons were rendered. The location of each place was then mapped to the bounds of one of the 14 regions. Of the 383 candidate locations, 335 fell explicitly within one of the regions. To make use of as much of the data as possible there was some consideration that either the exact coordinates of the places in the Pleiades set or the boundaries might have been slightly off another method was employed. The centroid of each region was calculated and the remaining 48 places were mapped to the closest region to their location."},{"type":"heading","text":"Place Valence Assignment"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"All places are not the same. All housing is not the same. A mansion is not a hovel. All neighborhoods are not alike. A neighborhood near a park is not a neighborhood near a factory. Aside from unsubtle concerns about keeping \"undesirables\" out of elite enclaves mentioned in the 1937 HOLC map details of San Francisco, practical matters concerning housing quality and environmental factors were present in the grading appraisals of the 96 districts."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For there 383 places identified in the Pleiades data each was assigned at least one place type. This was true for 82% of the locations. In the data set there were 67 distinct place types. The most prevalent were places of worship; temples and churches. To determine the possible value of a region of Rome, each type was assigned a positive, negative, or neutral scoring."},{"type":"heading","text":"Positive Valence Place Types"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These 30 place types represented public capital investments, elite residential zoning, or premium public amenities that boost area desirability."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Elite Residential & Real Estate Accumulation: townhouse, villa, palace, palace-complex, estate."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Pristine Utility Networks: aqueduct, bath, fountain, spring, cistern."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Environmental Buffers & Green Spaces: garden-hortus, grove, gymnasium, plain."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Institutional, Civic, & Economic Power Centers: forum, basilica, plaza, monument, arch, plaza, building (standard legal structures), architectural complex."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Monumental Leisure & Entertainment: theatre, amphitheatre, circus, stadion."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"High-Status Cultural Infrastructure: sanctuary, shrine, temple-2, altar."},{"type":"heading","text":"Negative Valence Place Types"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These 15 place types represented industrial pollution, low-status housing density, transit crowding, or militarized surveillance that would have triggered redlining thresholds."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Environmental Degradation & Nuisances: production (smoky manufacturing/tanneries), quarry (heavy resource extraction)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Low-Status Housing Density: settlement (unregulated laborer shanties/migrant clusters)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Militarized Surveillance & Containment Grids: barracks, fort, fort-2, city-wall, city-gate, tower-defensive."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Exiled Necropolis Land Use (Taboo Fringes): tomb, cemetery, catacomb, tumulus."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Transit & Freight Logistics Congestion: port, horreum (freight shipping warehouses)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The remaining place types were coded as neutral."}]},{"key":"scoring","title":"Scoring, grades and cross-city results","paragraphCount":14,"tableCount":4,"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Grading the Regions of Rome"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"With these types labeled as positive, negative, or neutral they were then associated with each region. A composite score was then calculated as the count of positive places in a region minus the count of negative places in a region. The results in Table 1 were then standardized by dividing by the area of the region in kilometers. We see that region 10 is the most valuable region in Rome by far. This is followed by region 8 and region 4. At the other end of the scaling we have region 13 and region 14, both with negative scores."},{"type":"heading","text":"Table 1: Region Scores"},{"type":"table","headers":["Region","Region Area (km)","Positive Count","Negative Count","Positive Density","Negative Density","Net Investment Score"],"rows":[["1","2.23","10","8","4.484","3.587","0.897"],["2","1.52","10","8","6.579","5.263","1.316"],["3","0.37","9","2","24.324","5.405","18.919"],["4","0.26","28","9","107.692","34.615","73.077"],["5","2.21","26","13","11.765","5.882","5.882"],["6","1.81","13","11","7.182","6.077","1.105"],["7","0.96","23","4","23.958","4.167","19.792"],["8","0.28","26","1","92.857","3.571","89.286"],["9","1.58","28","6","17.722","3.797","13.924"],["10","0.15","18","1","120.000","6.667","113.333"],["11","0.31","15","0","48.387","0.000","48.387"],["12","0.68","11","0","16.176","0.000","16.176"],["13","0.62","3","6","4.839","9.677","-4.839"],["14","2.15","7","8","3.256","3.721","-0.465"]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"While this provides us a sense of the extremes of value it doesn’t exactly provide us insights into which regions would fall into the HOLC letter and color grades. The first attempt to address this involves normalizing the scores to a 0 to 100 value. This is done with the following calculation."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"4 brackets based on the quartile cut points were then applied, such that the following scores should correspond to the following letter grades:"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Normalized Score >= 75%  = HOLC Grade A"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Normalized score between 50% and 74% = HOLC Grade B"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Normalized score between 25% and 49% = HOLC Grade C"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Normalized score < 25% = HOLC Grade D"},{"type":"heading","text":"Table 2: Normalized Region Scores"},{"type":"table","headers":["Region","Net Investment Score","Normalized Net Investment Score","HOLC Letter Grade","HOLC Color"],"rows":[["1","0.897","0.049","D",""],["2","1.316","0.052","D",""],["3","18.919","0.201","D",""],["4","73.077","0.659","B",""],["5","5.882","0.091","D",""],["6","1.105","0.050","D",""],["7","19.792","0.208","D",""],["8","89.286","0.797","A",""],["9","13.924","0.159","D",""],["10","113.333","1.000","A",""],["11","48.387","0.450","C",""],["12","16.176","0.178","D",""],["13","-4.839","0.000","D",""],["14","-0.465","0.037","D",""]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In Table 2 we see these normalized results. With a heavy bias towards negative appraisal of the 14  regions. This is likely due to the skewing based on the size of a region. It tends to magnify the negatives and the positives. To account for this a final region-size agnostic score was calculated. It created a ratio of positive place types in a region relative to the total number of positive and negative types in that same region."},{"type":"heading","text":"Table 3: Size-Agnostic Region Scores"},{"type":"table","headers":["Region","Positive Count","Negative Count","Total Count","Positive Percentage","HOLC Letter Grade","HOLC Color"],"rows":[["1","10","8","18","55.56%","D",""],["2","10","8","18","55.56%","D",""],["3","9","2","11","81.82%","B",""],["4","28","9","37","75.68%","C",""],["5","26","13","39","66.67%","D",""],["6","13","11","24","54.17%","D",""],["7","23","4","27","85.19%","B",""],["8","26","1","27","96.30%","A",""],["9","28","6","34","82.35%","B",""],["10","18","1","19","94.74%","A",""],["11","15","0","15","100.00%","A",""],["12","11","0","11","100.00%","A",""],["13","3","6","9","33.33%","D",""],["14","7","8","15","46.67%","D",""]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"In Table 3 we see this bias towards lower approximated HOLC grades has been reduced. When we compare these grade ratios to the appraisals in the 1937 San Francisco data in Table 4 we see that only the normalised region score accounts for some reflection of San Francisco. Even then it is only seen in the A grade count. In both the normalized and agnostic garings there was a consistent bias to underestimate C grades and overestimate D grades relative to the San Francisco scores. Some of this can be accounted for by the drastically smaller number of regions in Augustan Rome. Comparing 96 districts to 14 introduces the possibility of a higher level of variance in the data. Were the granularity of the San Francisco regions reduced, or the granularity of the 14 Roman regions increased, we would likely see a higher convergence between the two locations and their grade appraisals."},{"type":"heading","text":"Table 4: San Francisco vs. Rome HOLC Grade Estimations"},{"type":"table","headers":["HOLC Grade","SF Pct","Rome Normalized Pct","Normalized Difference","Rome Size Agnostic Pct","Agnostic Difference"],"rows":[["A","13.54%","14.29%","0.74%","28.57%","15.03%"],["B","31.25%","7.14%","-24.11%","21.43%","-9.82%"],["C","27.08%","7.14%","-19.94%","7.14%","-19.94%"],["D","17.71%","71.43%","53.72%","42.86%","25.15%"]]}]},{"key":"limitations","title":"Conclusion and limitations","paragraphCount":5,"tableCount":0,"blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This paper aims to examine whether the mechanisms of urban segregation visible in twentieth-century San Francisco could also be traced in the topography of Augustan Rome, despite the two societies sharing no direct historical contact. Following the comparative logic outlined by Bloch, the absence of any direct influence between Rome and San Francisco was treated as the condition that makes the comparison meaningful. That is, if a similar pattern of resource-based stratification appears in both locations independently, that convergence points toward a structural feature of hierarchical societies rather than a borrowed practice or coincidence."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The paper first argues that both societies built formal systems for categorizing their populations. Rome divided through the evolving distinction between citizen and outsider, and later between honestiores and humiliores, while the United States did so through HOLC's racialized grading criteria. Both then used explicit control over infrastructure and capital to convert that ranking into legalized inequality. In Rome, this took the form of differential access to aqueduct water, private fountains, and bath complexes; in San Francisco, it took the form of mortgage capital, insurance, and investment directed by the 1937 Residential Security Map."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"To test whether this pattern was present, the paper mapped the Pleiades dataset of archaeological sites onto Filippo Coarelli's fourteen administrative regions of Augustan Rome, coding site types as positive, negative, or neutral markers of neighborhood investment. Finally it translates those counts into HOLC-style letter grades using both a normalized and a size-agnostic scoring method. These approximated Roman grades compared against the actual 1937 San Francisco distribution demonstrated a directional resemblance, most significantly in the top-tier (A) districts, but also showing a bias toward over-assigning low grades in Rome, likely a product of the coarser granularity of fourteen regions compared to San Francisco's ninety-six. This suggests that while the underlying system of resource-driven stratification does appear to recur across the two cities, the specific quantitative comparison is sensitive to scale, and a higher-resolution subdivision of Rome would likely sharpen the convergence. Together, these findings support the paper's central claim: segregation is not a phenomenon unique to any era's race or class, but a definite structural outcome of how hierarchy in societies allocate infrastructure and capital. Rome's aqueducts and San Francisco's mortgage lines, separated by two thousand years and drawn from entirely different ideological vocabularies, both functioned as instruments for converting social rank into physical geography."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"One area this paper does not include is examining funerary practice as another indicator of social stratification in Rome. Roman burial custom was strictly regulated, as the Twelve Tables prohibited burial within the city walls, pushing tombs to the periphery along roads like the Appian Way. The resulting hierarchy of funerary practices, from public columbaria to monumental family tombs, offers another possible independent perspective through which rank was inscribed onto the urban landscape. Because tomb type, inscription density, and location relative to major roads can all be found to some extent in the Pleiades data, future work could extend the current framework to test whether the same regions graded favorably for water access and elite housing also show a concentration of high-status funerary monuments. If such a correlation held, it would strengthen the paper's central argument by showing that Roman spatial hierarchy was reinforced not only through access to water and housing, but also through the treatment and location of the dead. This presents a supplement worth exploring against the way cemetery access and burial segregation have been documented as a component of redlined American neighborhoods."}]}];
